      "tool" : {
        "driver" : {
          "informationUri" : "https://github.com/g-cqd/fcheck",
          "name" : "fcheck",
          "rules" : [
            {
              "defaultConfiguration" : {
                "level" : "warning"
              },
              "fullDescription" : {
                "text" : "When a function acquires a handle it must, on every path out (return, throw, end of body), close it, hand it to something that owns it, or return it. The paths include the ones a `try` takes when it throws and the ones a `defer` runs on.\n\nAcquisitions are `open`, `FileDescriptor.open`, `socket`, `dup`, `opendir`, `fopen` and the like, and any function of the corpus that returns one. Handing on is passing the handle to a call that is not a plain system call on it, storing it, or returning it. Add to the list in `.fcheck.json` under `resources`.\n\nFix: close it in a `defer` placed right after the acquisition, or close it in the branch that fails."
              },
              "id" : "leak-on-exit",
              "properties" : {
                "evidence" : "L0",
                "shape" : "pairing"
              },
              "shortDescription" : {
                "text" : "a descriptor or handle is not closed, handed on or returned on some path out of the function"
              }
            },
            {
              "defaultConfiguration" : {
                "level" : "warning"
              },
              "fullDescription" : {
                "text" : "Replacing a file atomically is four steps in an order: write the new bytes, flush them, rename over the old file, flush the directory. A rename of unflushed bytes can leave an empty or partial file after a crash. A rename not followed by a directory flush can bring the old file back, which matters most where the file holds a single-use token.\n\nA flush is `fsync`, `fdatasync`, `fcntl(_, F_FULLFSYNC)`, a `sync` method, or any call whose name contains `flush`. Add names under `flush` in `.fcheck.json`.\n\nFix: flush the descriptor before the rename, and the directory after it, or call the helper that does."
              },
              "id" : "replace-not-durable",
              "properties" : {
                "evidence" : "L0",
                "shape" : "ordering"
              },
              "shortDescription" : {
                "text" : "a rename replaces a file whose bytes, or whose directory, were not flushed"
              }
            },
            {
              "defaultConfiguration" : {
                "level" : "warning"
              },
              "fullDescription" : {
                "text" : "`String(decoding: bytes.prefix(n), as: UTF8.self)` replaces an incomplete character at the cut with U+FFFD, so valid text printed by a program is shown to a reader with replacement characters in it. The same holds for a suffix and for any slice whose bounds are counts of bytes.\n\nFix: move the cut back to a character boundary (a byte that is not `0b10xxxxxx`), or decode the whole buffer and cut the string."
              },
              "id" : "cut-then-decode",
              "properties" : {
                "evidence" : "L0",
                "shape" : "bounds"
              },
              "shortDescription" : {
                "text" : "bytes cut at a count are decoded as text, splitting a character that straddles the cut"
              }
            }
          ],
          "version" : "0.2.0"
        }
      }
    }
  ],
  "version" : "2.1.0"
